{"prdMarkdown":"# PRD: User Profile Avatar Uploads\n\n## Problem\nCurrently, Ledger API users cannot personalize their accounts or visually distinguish profiles because the service lacks support for avatar image uploads.\n\n## Goals\n- Enable users to upload an image to serve as their profile avatar.\n- Persist the avatar file reference against the user account record.\n- Enforce reasonable file size and format validation to ensure only valid images are processed.\n- Serve or display the avatar across relevant user interfaces (`templates/index.html`, `static/style.css`).\n\n## Non-Goals\n- Client-side or server-side image cropping or manipulation (deferred post-v1).\n- Storing images in external cloud object storage (v1 will store files on disk).\n- Generating multiple responsive resolutions or thumbnails.\n\n## User Impact\n- Users will be able to upload custom image files to represent their profile in the application.\n- Fast failure feedback when attempting to upload unsupported file types or oversized files.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- What exact maximum file size limit (e.g., 2MB, 5MB) and specific image MIME/file types (e.g., JPEG, PNG, WebP, GIF) should be permitted?\n- Should previous avatar files be automatically deleted from disk when a user uploads a new avatar or deletes their profile, or should historical uploads be retained?","trdMarkdown":"# TRD: User Profile Avatar Uploads\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- **Model Changes**: Add an `avatar_path` column to the user model in `models.py` to store the relative disk path or filename.\n- **Route & Upload Handling**: In `app.py`, establish a multipart file upload handler. Implement `werkzeug.utils.secure_filename` alongside UUID generation for unique filename creation to prevent collisions and path traversal attacks.\n- **Configuration**: Set `MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH` in `app.py` to block oversized uploads before consuming server memory.\n- **File Storage**: Store files in a dedicated local directory accessible by the Flask application and Gunicorn.\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **File Validation & Spoofing**: `requirements.txt` currently lacks an image processing or byte-inspection library (such as `Pillow` or `python-magic`). Relying strictly on client extensions or `Content-Type` headers leaves the endpoint vulnerable to MIME spoofing.\n- **Schema Migrations**: `models.py` needs a new column, but no migration framework (like `Flask-Migrate` or `Alembic`) is present in `requirements.txt`.\n- **Orphaned Files**: Modifying or removing avatars in `app.py` without cleanup logic will cause unreferenced image files to accumulate on disk.\n- **Multi-Worker & Container Persistence**: In environments running `gunicorn==21.2.0`, local disk storage may not persist across container restarts or sync across distributed instances without a shared persistent volume.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- How should database schema migrations be executed for adding the avatar path column to `models.py` (e.g., introducing Flask-Migrate/Alembic vs. executing raw SQL)?\n- Should an image validation library like `Pillow` or `python-magic` be added to `requirements.txt` to inspect magic bytes and prevent MIME spoofing, or is file extension filtering sufficient?\n- Where on disk should uploaded avatars be stored, and will the deployment environment (e.g., multi-worker Gunicorn or containerization) support persistent local disk storage or require a shared volume mount?\n\n## Estimate\n- Nominal: 2-3 days\n- Worst Case: Up to 7 days (if introducing a formal migration framework, storage volume orchestration, and image byte validation)"}